<section class="flint-intro" aria-label="About FLINT">
  <p>We study the mathematical foundations of learning with nonlinear models, connecting approximation, optimization, and generalization to the design and understanding of modern learning systems.</p>
  <p>Led by <a href="index.html">Fanghui Liu</a> at Shanghai Jiao Tong University, FLINT brings together research in learning theory and the mechanisms of large language models. Fanghui holds a joint appointment at the <a href="https://ins.sjtu.edu.cn/topics/overview">Institute of Natural Sciences</a> and the <a href="https://math.sjtu.edu.cn/">School of Mathematical Sciences</a>.</p>
  <p class="flint-principle">Our focus is learning efficiency: understanding nonlinearity mathematically, and using that understanding to develop precise and efficient learning methods.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="research-themes">
  <h2 id="research-themes">Research themes</h2>
  <div class="flint-theme-grid">
    <article class="flint-theme">
      <p class="flint-kicker">Mathematical foundations</p>
      <h3>Learning theory &amp; nonlinear approximation</h3>
      <p>What can nonlinear models represent and learn, and at what statistical and computational cost?</p>
      <ul>
        <li>High-dimensional approximation, generalization, and function spaces.</li>
        <li>Computational&ndash;statistical gaps and learning efficiency.</li>
        <li>Generalization under scaling and effective model capacity.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="flint-theme">
      <p class="flint-kicker">Mechanisms of modern learning</p>
      <h3>Understanding large language models</h3>
      <p>How do training, fine-tuning, and reasoning work, and how can their mechanisms guide better methods?</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Pre-training scaling laws, training dynamics, and stability.</li>
        <li>Fine-tuning: when, where, and how much to adapt.</li>
        <li>Reasoning, Lean formalization, and AI for machine learning theory.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="selected-research">
  <h2 id="selected-research">Selected research &amp; projects</h2>
  <p class="flint-section-note">A starting point for our research, drawing on work by Fanghui Liu and collaborators. The full publication list remains on the <a href="publications.html">personal research website</a>.</p>
  <div class="flint-work-list">
    <article class="flint-work">
      <h3>Learning theory and nonlinear approximation</h3>
      <p>Function-space perspectives on approximation and learning.</p>
      <p class="flint-work-links"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.18769">JMLR 2024</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01585">NeurIPS 2025</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="flint-work">
      <h3>From theory to fine-tuning</h3>
      <p>Connecting mathematical understanding with efficient adaptation of large language models.</p>
      <p class="flint-work-links"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01235">ICML 2025 &middot; Oral</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="flint-work">
      <h3>Reasoning and formalization</h3>
      <p>Research-level reasoning and formal foundations for probability, statistics, and machine learning in Lean.</p>
      <p class="flint-work-links"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19842">ICLR 2026</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02285">ICML 2026</a><a href="https://statsmllib.github.io/">StatsMLlib</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="flint-join" aria-labelledby="connect-with-flint">
